WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.237
Preflight resilience begins with checking navigation status, home point, compass or heading consistency, maps, warnings, firmware state, and expected flight modes according to approved procedures.

00:00:12.237 --> 00:00:17.548
An unexplained anomaly before launch is a reason to stop.

00:00:17.548 --> 00:00:22.396
Plan a simple route that can be understood visually.

00:00:22.396 --> 00:00:36.019
Maintain visual line of sight as required and appropriate, identify stable landmarks, and avoid placing the aircraft where loss of automated position would immediately threaten people or obstacles.

00:00:36.019 --> 00:00:48.256
Manual-control readiness means training for the aircraft's degraded behavior in authorized safe conditions, understanding mode changes, and knowing the controls without improvisation during an incident.

00:00:48.256 --> 00:00:52.181
It does not mean experimenting with interference.

00:00:52.181 --> 00:00:54.259
Use conservative automation.

00:00:54.259 --> 00:01:00.493
Verify each waypoint, route boundary, home point, return setting, and emergency area.

00:01:00.493 --> 00:01:09.036
Define abort criteria for navigation warnings, cue disagreement, drifting, mode changes, unexpected route behavior, or low confidence.

00:01:09.036 --> 00:01:15.270
Identify landing areas along the mission, not only at the launch point.

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:19.657
Consider wind, terrain, people, property, access, and battery.

00:01:19.657 --> 00:01:27.276
A nearby controlled landing may be safer than a long automated return through uncertain navigation.

00:01:27.276 --> 00:01:33.510
Document the plan, warnings, receiver status, visual references, settings, and crew roles.

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:43.438
Resilience comes from independent cues, practiced decisions, and preserved margin, not from assuming that more automation will overcome unreliable inputs.

00:01:43.438 --> 00:01:48.749
Rehearse decisions through checklists and tabletop discussion using hypothetical warnings.

00:01:48.749 --> 00:01:58.677
Crews can practice communication, automation disengagement, landing selection, and reporting without transmitting interference or creating risk to real navigation systems.
